MergePlan / MergeReport / RecordIdentity each provide to_json(&self) -> Json.

JSON fields:
MergePlan: added, updated, unchanged, conflicts, keptTrashed, keptActive,
  skippedDeleted, deletedInBackup, markersAdded, conflictCopiesAdded,
  conflictCopiesDropped, mediaAdded, mediaMissing, mediaConflicts.
RecordIdentity (skippedDeleted / deletedInBackup elements): recordId, createdAt.
mediaConflicts elements: assetId strings.
MergeReport: plan, safetyBackupDir (nullable for no-op), mediaReport.

Container:
10-byte magic SGBAKENC\x00\x01; u32 LE JSON header length (max 64 KiB);
UTF-8 JSON {format,formatVersion,kdf:{alg,version,m,t,p,salt},
aead:{alg,noncePrefix,chunkSize},plaintextBytes}.
format=shiguang-backup-encrypted; formatVersion=1; KDF=Argon2id v19;
salt=16 random bytes in canonical base64; AEAD=XChaCha20-Poly1305;
noncePrefix=19 random bytes in canonical base64; chunkSize=1048576.
Ciphertext chunks have a 16-byte detached tag appended. Chunk sizes/count and
last flag are inferred from authenticated plaintextBytes; exact outer length
is checked before KDF, and counters are bounded to u32 (never wrap).
Nonce = prefix(19) || u32 BE counter || last(0/1).
AAD = all framing/header bytes, including magic and length, for every chunk.
Inner plaintext is the complete Unit-0.5 container, encryption.mode=none.
Full inner verification precedes plaintext publication. Existing destinations
are preserved. Temporary plaintext stays under the data root (store wrappers)
or a private plaintext file in the OS temp directory (store-free verify); output partial stays in the
target directory. Unix backup/plaintext files are created mode 0600.
Closed file handles precede publication, rename, unlink (Windows compatible).
KDF accepts the SPEC bounds and rejects cryptographically inconsistent lane /
memory combinations via Argon2 parameter validation. Keys and passwords are
zeroized; passwords are never used in paths or diagnostic formatting.

Merge safety:
Exclusive Store + write lock; complete backup verify; same pure merge for plan
and commit; serialized result re-parsed via State::from_json before writing.
Strict ID validation also runs on open: record/conflict IDs globally unique;
photo use IDs identify one logical owner/asset/hash, shared across snapshots;
no duplicate photoUseId inside a snapshot; seq >= used numeric suffixes.
Safe copy backups/pre-merge-<id> contains state.json (when present) and SAFETY.json
listing new media. .merge-journal.json lists only planned new originals and
owned media/.tmp-merge-* files plus the commit state hash. New media are streamed,
rehashed, fsynced, then renamed; atomic state replacement is the commit point.
Identical state bytes get one trailing newline when media repair requires a
commit, so journal recovery cannot mistake the old state for committed state.
Open/open_exclusive roll back pre-commit media or clear a committed journal.
Recovery validates all paths before deleting any, caps journal size at 64 MiB
and path count at twice the existing entry limit. Existing media are never
replaced. Reconciliation follows a successful merge; repeated import preserves
state bytes and creates no additional conflict copies.

SPEC additions/deviations and reasons:
1. merge_states returns Result rather than an unchecked tuple, to reject invalid
   IDs / exhausted sequence numbers without panics. It remains pure and uses no IO.
2. Optional schema-v2 mergeOrigins (default empty) records source-to-local record
   and photo ID aliases. Without persistent aliases a reimport after collision
   would duplicate records or conflict snapshots. Re-export retains canonical
   identity, including markers for previously remapped and purged records.
3. Additional MergeAfterCommit fault point and kth-visit injection cover both
   sides of the commit point and later media/crypto chunks.
4. Existing-test conflict above prevents claiming the requested all-green status.
   No assertion was silently weakened and no legacy JSON projection was added.

CLI:
export-backup <dest> [--encrypt] [--kdf-fast] [--password-stdin]
verify-backup <file> [--password-stdin] (store-free, including encrypted inputs)
plan-merge <file> [--password-stdin]
merge-backup <file> --yes [--password-stdin]
restore-backup <file> --yes [--password-stdin]
Only SHIGUANG_BACKUP_PASSWORD or --password-stdin supplies secret input.
Password-valued command arguments are rejected without echo, before store open.

